target: internal/record/record.go
verify: go test ./internal/record/ -count=1 -run 'TestDroppedGates'
expect: FAIL: TestDroppedGatesRendersATrueStatusLikePython
why:    the status reaches a report through review-stats.py's f-string, so the Python's rendering is str(). fmt.Sprint agrees with str() on strings and on JSON's numbers but NOT on its booleans: str(True) is "True". `status: true` is reachable for exactly the reason `status: false` is — cmd_finish never validates the vocabulary — so this is a parity break in the one place an operator reads, the alarm line naming the gate's problem. The parity gate found it; the unit tests could not have, because they assert against a reading of the Python rather than the Python itself.
--- old
	case bool:
		if v {
			return "True"
		}
		return "False"
--- new
	case bool:
		return fmt.Sprint(v)
